1
00:00:07,597 --> 00:00:08,625
[SPEAKER_01]: You see me as a backdrop?

2
00:00:37,949 --> 00:00:39,770
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wondering if you could describe nibbāna.

3
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:41,310
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that?

4
00:00:42,091 --> 00:00:42,731
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your aim?

5
00:00:43,791 --> 00:00:44,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Your aim is nibbāna.

6
00:00:44,932 --> 00:00:47,353
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

7
00:00:48,693 --> 00:00:52,375
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean if somebody gives that as an answer to a question?

8
00:00:53,395 --> 00:00:54,876
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, my aim is nibbāna.

9
00:00:55,496 --> 00:00:55,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

10
00:00:57,397 --> 00:00:59,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing, actually.

11
00:00:59,798 --> 00:01:03,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding correctly what nibbāna is, means you would be at least a sotāpanna.

12
00:01:05,931 --> 00:01:11,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully developing that understanding means, yeah, you have now developed nibbāna, as in became an arahant.

13
00:01:12,335 --> 00:01:18,639
[SPEAKER_01]: As to what it is, well, as the suttas would describe it, it's absence of greed, aversion and delusion.

14
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:28,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Complete absence, in the sense of the possibility of greed, aversion or delusion is impossible.

15
00:01:28,986 --> 00:01:35,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So the mind has fully understood it and fully got rid of it.

16
00:01:36,461 --> 00:01:38,042
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a place that one goes to.

17
00:01:38,963 --> 00:01:39,663
[SPEAKER_01]: No, exactly.

18
00:01:40,043 --> 00:01:40,384
[SPEAKER_01]: It isn't.

19
00:01:41,404 --> 00:01:57,875
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... it's not a... it's not a magical realm that kind of gets... one enters upon if... upon some certain practice of a special meditation technique or something like that.

20
00:01:58,755 --> 00:02:01,577
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a place you go into and replace what you know with it.

21
00:02:02,536 --> 00:02:08,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really what you know now, your experience as it is, what you have now.

22
00:02:08,077 --> 00:02:09,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Remove greed, aversion, delusion.

23
00:02:10,038 --> 00:02:14,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Remove possibility of greed, possibility of aversion, possibility of delusion from it.

24
00:02:14,879 --> 00:02:16,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what nibbāna becomes.

25
00:02:16,899 --> 00:02:20,200
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what nibbāna is.

26
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,381
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we would say cooling down, literally.

27
00:02:26,401 --> 00:02:29,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Cooling down of the fires of passion, anger and so on.

28
00:02:31,791 --> 00:02:41,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cool, cool this thing down, this experience, this life you have, you cool it down to the point of being irreversible.

29
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:46,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot go back and be entered again by greed, aversion or delusion.

30
00:02:46,243 --> 00:02:53,129
[SPEAKER_01]: It's been fully, fully frozen to the point of these three things dying out and now they cannot reappear.

31
00:02:54,310 --> 00:02:56,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's, that's a developed nibbāna.

32
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:00,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see that in order to cool something down,

33
00:03:01,210 --> 00:03:08,693
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have it, you need to acknowledge it, you need to admit it, you need to see where the work in cooling it down lies.

34
00:03:09,693 --> 00:03:17,717
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not replacing it with some new world, a new realm, or new meditation experience.

35
00:03:18,477 --> 00:03:20,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You're actually fully understanding what you already have.

36
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:21,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't need anything new.

37
00:03:21,939 --> 00:03:24,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever it's necessary for nibbāna, you already have it.

38
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:27,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody already has it.

39
00:03:27,961 --> 00:03:30,442
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would refer to it as an element of nibbāna.

40
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:32,307
[SPEAKER_01]: In everyone's experience.

41
00:03:33,228 --> 00:03:36,769
[SPEAKER_01]: As in possibility of developing the phenomenon which is nibbāna.

42
00:03:36,849 --> 00:03:38,990
[SPEAKER_01]: That means, element of nibbāna is present.

43
00:03:40,530 --> 00:03:42,051
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about belief.

44
00:03:42,511 --> 00:03:43,291
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, no.

45
00:03:43,411 --> 00:03:49,133
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you see, when people took it as a belief, that's when they started saying, well, we are already in nibbāna, because nibbāna is already there.

46
00:03:49,794 --> 00:03:54,635
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you fail to see the difference between the possibility and, like, actual development of something.

47
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:56,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, nibbāna is there as a possibility.

48
00:03:59,097 --> 00:03:59,257
[SPEAKER_01]: But,

49
00:04:00,390 --> 00:04:05,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying that you have developed nibbāna means you need to have it as your actual experience.

50
00:04:05,313 --> 00:04:11,418
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you need to do the work of getting rid of greed, aversion, delusion, ill-will, hatred, sensuality, all of that.

51
00:04:12,538 --> 00:04:17,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nibbāna is the perfected human being, and it's a perfected...

52
00:04:17,482 --> 00:04:29,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much, but then, see, perfected human being, basically, it's certainly a perfection, but you wouldn't refer to that person as a human being anymore, because human,

53
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:39,484
[SPEAKER_01]: It means going with the nature, going with the grain, going with the current, going with desires, going with the cravings and so on.

54
00:04:39,625 --> 00:04:42,906
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what makes humans human.

55
00:04:43,026 --> 00:04:45,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you actually... you cease to be human.

56
00:04:46,348 --> 00:04:50,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant is not human anymore.

57
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,871
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's the designation you would use for a human.

58
00:04:54,512 --> 00:04:58,914
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, overall, sure, as an individual, he still partakes as a being in the human realm.

59
00:05:01,675 --> 00:05:12,038
[SPEAKER_01]: But really, what makes human human are these things that you sort of undo and relinquish as an arahant, as somebody who is enlightened.

60
00:05:15,399 --> 00:05:16,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Overcoming your humanity.

61
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:18,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, pretty much, yeah.

62
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,881
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to overcome the immorality and non-virtue.

63
00:05:23,081 --> 00:05:25,102
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you need to overcome that as well.

64
00:05:25,582 --> 00:05:28,383
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're not really measurable by any human standards.

65
00:05:29,552 --> 00:05:34,497
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not like a good human or a bad human, a good person or a bad... No, he's not a person anymore.

66
00:05:34,517 --> 00:05:34,917
[SPEAKER_01]: He's done.

67
00:05:36,579 --> 00:05:45,428
[SPEAKER_01]: He's abandoned, relinquished the ownership of the slightest sort of appropriation of his own senses, his own aggregates.

68
00:05:50,203 --> 00:05:53,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is also called the non... what?

69
00:05:53,706 --> 00:05:56,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-appropriation in English, in other...

70
00:05:56,468 --> 00:05:58,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

71
00:05:58,330 --> 00:05:59,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-appropriation.

72
00:06:02,473 --> 00:06:03,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the undetermined.

73
00:06:04,255 --> 00:06:05,176
[SPEAKER_01]: The undetermined, yeah.

74
00:06:05,856 --> 00:06:14,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the things that are determined for a human, for an unenlightened, unenlightened being, are things that are determined by greed, aversion, delusion.

75
00:06:16,903 --> 00:06:20,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove that and there is nothing determining things as mine anymore.

76
00:06:21,384 --> 00:06:23,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, that experience is the undetermined.

77
00:06:23,685 --> 00:06:26,526
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience of the undetermined.

78
00:06:28,466 --> 00:06:30,267
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a real experience as such.

79
00:06:30,647 --> 00:06:34,368
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why nibbāna is a thing, a phenomena, a dhamma.

80
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:43,691
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't really show people that nibbāna.

81
00:06:43,971 --> 00:06:45,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you show them nibbāna?

82
00:06:45,272 --> 00:06:45,332
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

83
00:06:45,472 --> 00:06:46,312
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

84
00:06:47,048 --> 00:06:55,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can guide them to it, but even that will not necessarily result in them getting to it, unless they have made the effort themselves.

85
00:06:56,156 --> 00:07:11,868
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can point at it, you can describe it, you can lay out the way that somebody can practice towards it, but unless they practice...

86
00:07:11,889 --> 00:07:15,532
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like getting real, getting absolutely real.

87
00:07:15,852 --> 00:07:16,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

88
00:07:17,268 --> 00:07:19,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Being honest with your situation.

89
00:07:19,509 --> 00:07:22,471
[SPEAKER_00]: It's up to me, it's not... Oh, yeah, absolutely, yeah.

90
00:07:23,032 --> 00:07:26,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not just being... like being honest with the situation, but also recognizing...

91
00:07:27,014 --> 00:07:29,176
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would refer to it as measure the task.

92
00:07:30,216 --> 00:07:37,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a person would go and listen to like a... whatever, somebody he deems wise, somebody he deems that he can help him.

93
00:07:37,841 --> 00:07:42,324
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he would ponder on the meaning of what's being said and what needs to be done.

94
00:07:43,165 --> 00:07:46,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Once he starts grasping the meaning, he can see, oh, okay, this is...

95
00:07:46,563 --> 00:07:47,303
[SPEAKER_01]: The amount of effort.

96
00:07:47,383 --> 00:07:50,285
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what I need to do to, like, measure the task.

97
00:07:51,725 --> 00:07:54,567
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is absolutely necessary in order to be able to do it.

98
00:07:55,727 --> 00:08:00,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people don't... they kind of omit that whole thing of, hold on, what does this actually mean?

99
00:08:01,590 --> 00:08:02,690
[SPEAKER_01]: It might make me feel good.

100
00:08:02,710 --> 00:08:07,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my making me... it's my... it's... it might be very inspiring for me, but do I understand what it means?

101
00:08:09,213 --> 00:08:15,696
[SPEAKER_01]: When I read the suttas or when I face instruction that's sort of directing me towards nibbāna, am I aware of... of... of the actual

102
00:08:17,318 --> 00:08:19,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Meaning of it, as in, like, what exactly I need to do.

103
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:23,162
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people are not encouraged to think like that.

104
00:08:23,323 --> 00:08:25,964
[SPEAKER_01]: It's most like, okay, you're inspired, that's great.

105
00:08:26,565 --> 00:08:43,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, here is a meditation technique that you just do mechanically, and then the experience you have, if it's substantial enough, as in, like, if it affects you significantly enough, then we can interpret it as nibbāna, or as something leading to nibbāna.

106
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:46,119
[SPEAKER_01]: But nibbāna is not an interpretation.

107
00:08:47,559 --> 00:08:55,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Any, basically, any part of developing wisdom does not partake in interpretation, certainly not by external terms.

108
00:08:56,766 --> 00:09:01,970
[SPEAKER_01]: It partakes in direct understanding of the meaning of it, which then results in direct freedom from suffering.

109
00:09:02,110 --> 00:09:04,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And hence, that's the greatest verification of your wisdom.

110
00:09:05,453 --> 00:09:09,376
[SPEAKER_01]: You know you're right, because you cannot suffer amidst suffering.

111
00:09:09,456 --> 00:09:11,097
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot engage in your will.

112
00:09:11,197 --> 00:09:12,858
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot engage in sensuality.

113
00:09:13,719 --> 00:09:16,461
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot engage in craving, or distraction, or delusion.

114
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:25,269
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if the whole world says, no, that's wrong, you know that they're wrong, because you are the one free from suffering.

115
00:09:25,289 --> 00:09:33,799
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahant, anāgāmī, sakadāgāmī, sotāpanna, basically, dead ones, they go... Well, arahant is completely developed against the grain.

116
00:09:34,553 --> 00:09:39,075
[SPEAKER_01]: against the grain of nature and the outputs of the mind and intentions and cravings and feelings and so on.

117
00:09:39,635 --> 00:09:43,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpanna is somebody who got... he understood it.

118
00:09:43,797 --> 00:09:46,298
[SPEAKER_01]: He understood what that is, but he hasn't fully developed it.

119
00:09:46,798 --> 00:09:51,840
[SPEAKER_01]: He hasn't fully went upstream, so to speak.

120
00:09:53,841 --> 00:09:59,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, it's not like a theory or ideology that you're sort of...

121
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:02,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Justifying and making it like an argument proof or something.

122
00:10:02,987 --> 00:10:06,229
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's a directly verifiable in your experience.

123
00:10:06,309 --> 00:10:06,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

124
00:10:06,609 --> 00:10:08,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have the criteria.

125
00:10:09,131 --> 00:10:10,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering and freedom from it.

126
00:10:11,112 --> 00:10:12,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality and freedom from it.

127
00:10:12,772 --> 00:10:13,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Ill will and freedom from it.

128
00:10:14,373 --> 00:10:15,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're free from those things.

129
00:10:15,714 --> 00:10:23,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you've been running away from those things, or you've been hiding, or sort of trying to manage the circumstances, so you don't get challenged externally.

130
00:10:24,238 --> 00:10:24,358
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

131
00:10:24,498 --> 00:10:28,680
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free from those things, because amidst those things, the mind cannot be overwhelmed by them.

132
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:29,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't engage.

133
00:10:30,602 --> 00:10:32,302
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even a matter of restraint anymore.

134
00:10:32,403 --> 00:10:33,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes impossible.

135
00:10:34,023 --> 00:10:38,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Once understanding has fully taken place, it becomes impossible to suffer amid suffering.

136
00:10:39,985 --> 00:10:46,326
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as I said, that that is the the greatest verification a person can have, that they've been practicing rightly.

137
00:11:00,950 --> 00:11:02,511
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no verification from others?

138
00:11:04,151 --> 00:11:05,152
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

139
00:11:05,172 --> 00:11:05,292
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

140
00:11:05,932 --> 00:11:06,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Well done.

141
00:11:06,432 --> 00:11:06,572
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

142
00:11:07,633 --> 00:11:23,440
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the others can verify it as they did in the suttas, basically, not by, yes, I see the magic blue light aura around his head, he's an arahant, but by talking to that person, interrogating them, to the point of discerning the subtle meanings, and they were like, yeah.

143
00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:29,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Because an arahant would know what another arahant needs, has to have understood beforehand.

144
00:11:31,088 --> 00:11:35,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he would know that, which means, through questioning, he could see the extent of other person's understanding.

145
00:11:36,454 --> 00:11:38,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's... that was also a way of progress as well.

146
00:11:39,778 --> 00:11:47,886
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would often encourage that in the suttas, saying the monks would sort of interrogate each other, not like for the sake of argument, but for the sake of pressing for the meaning.

147
00:11:48,627 --> 00:11:50,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't leave anything unturned.

148
00:11:51,659 --> 00:11:59,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you'll be drilling and delving and basically making effort to understand and then being questioned by your... what's the translation?...

149
00:11:59,681 --> 00:12:01,242
[SPEAKER_01]: by spiritual companions and so on.

150
00:12:01,682 --> 00:12:03,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it's like, have you understood this?

151
00:12:04,003 --> 00:12:04,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you understood?

152
00:12:04,763 --> 00:12:10,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you thought you did, well, you will then be able to convey it to somebody else who did understand it.

153
00:12:12,156 --> 00:12:24,885
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, again, if you thought you did, but you haven't, this questioning can actually spur you to make further effort, because you realize, oh, okay, I haven't been considering this, or that, or whatever the questions contain.

154
00:12:27,466 --> 00:12:32,110
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, you can see that most of the modern practice today, when it comes to Buddhism meditation, has nothing to do with it.

155
00:12:33,290 --> 00:12:39,975
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just watch your nostrils, cross your legs, sit in lotus, and that's it, and it will happen, whatever.

156
00:12:43,254 --> 00:12:49,736
[SPEAKER_00]: From what you just said, it seems that discussing the dhamma is useful.

157
00:12:52,057 --> 00:12:52,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Go on.

158
00:12:52,757 --> 00:12:54,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Shall I continue the sentence?

159
00:12:54,558 --> 00:12:54,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

160
00:12:54,778 --> 00:12:59,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Discussing the dhamma is useful, provided that at least one of those people has understood it.

161
00:13:00,419 --> 00:13:01,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, it's blind leading the blind.

162
00:13:02,860 --> 00:13:11,843
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, two unenlightened persons, people who haven't even understood what sotāpati is, they can discuss the dhamma all they want.

163
00:13:14,650 --> 00:13:15,911
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't result in knowledge.

164
00:13:16,091 --> 00:13:20,574
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, like, you pair two ignorances together, it's not going to amount to knowledge.

165
00:13:21,715 --> 00:13:24,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Because Putujan's experience is top-to-bottom ignorance.

166
00:13:25,757 --> 00:13:31,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot accidentally stumble upon or even find a right reference towards that ignorance.

167
00:13:31,841 --> 00:13:38,886
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, the absolutely necessary condition for arising on the right view is utterance of another, of another who has understood it.

168
00:13:40,218 --> 00:13:42,599
[SPEAKER_01]: And, paired with that, they need to have the right attention.

169
00:13:42,719 --> 00:13:50,343
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, they need to know, basically, how to hear it, not to dismiss it, how to attend to it, and how to practice that right utterance.

170
00:13:52,404 --> 00:14:00,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, once... See, like, for example, two sotāpānas could enable each other better in practice, if they were to discuss it.

171
00:14:01,308 --> 00:14:07,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it would just be reminding them of what they already know, but they haven't pressed it far enough, in terms of understanding.

172
00:14:08,853 --> 00:14:11,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even better, sotāpaṭṭhāna talking to an arahant.

173
00:14:13,095 --> 00:14:16,216
[SPEAKER_01]: But putujana toitu putujana, it can only remain putujana.

174
00:14:17,696 --> 00:14:21,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Or be dealing with mundane terms.

175
00:14:21,417 --> 00:14:22,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

176
00:14:22,498 --> 00:14:28,400
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be helping each other how to manage their suffering better.

177
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:31,341
[SPEAKER_01]: But sotāpaṭṭhāna is not management of suffering.

178
00:14:31,461 --> 00:14:33,082
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the first step of uprooting it.

179
00:14:34,882 --> 00:14:36,823
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you know exactly how to uproot it.

180
00:14:37,453 --> 00:14:45,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already freedom from suffering, you know, to a great degree.

181
00:14:47,982 --> 00:14:49,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what about speech?

182
00:14:50,064 --> 00:14:50,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Truthful speech?

183
00:14:53,887 --> 00:14:54,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Right speech?

184
00:15:00,305 --> 00:15:17,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you say, so you wouldn't say that discussing the dhamma or your concerns, your spiritual concerns, with other people who are also on the same kind of path, you wouldn't say that's right or noble?

185
00:15:19,612 --> 00:15:25,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you define noble by that which pertains to, like, sort of your higher, then it isn't, of course.

186
00:15:26,295 --> 00:15:28,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it can help people because

187
00:15:29,623 --> 00:15:40,689
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense of, eh... it can... it can make them make the effort on the personal level further by having an intelligent feedback, even if it's unadvertentional.

188
00:15:41,169 --> 00:15:46,612
[SPEAKER_01]: What they should not expect is that the conversation, the discussion itself, results in some great epiphany.

189
00:15:47,653 --> 00:15:57,638
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the same sense, you come across a new sutta, or you come across other wrong view, that then you actually understand better, and by doing so, you clarify what the right view is.

190
00:15:58,456 --> 00:16:07,825
[SPEAKER_01]: So, of course, that's all thanks to their personal effort towards understanding the nature of suffering and freedom from it.

191
00:16:07,845 --> 00:16:17,734
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, if there is... if the other person is equally sort of striving as you are, then that discussion can sort of just, well, at least hint at certain directions.

192
00:16:20,239 --> 00:16:22,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Of your reflection and of your contemplation.

193
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:23,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Good.

194
00:16:24,161 --> 00:16:26,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Both of those people say, well, I'm not enlightened.

195
00:16:26,702 --> 00:16:27,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you enlightened?

196
00:16:27,302 --> 00:16:28,022
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'm not enlightened.

197
00:16:28,042 --> 00:16:30,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's two unenlightened, honest unenlightened.

198
00:16:30,183 --> 00:16:31,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, now, yeah, exactly.

199
00:16:31,043 --> 00:16:32,104
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a difference between that.

200
00:16:32,144 --> 00:16:34,565
[SPEAKER_01]: You have sometimes people debating just because it's pleasant.

201
00:16:35,225 --> 00:16:38,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, let's have a discussion about it and just bounce ideas off each other.

202
00:16:39,227 --> 00:16:42,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I mean, that's utterly pointless.

203
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:59,289
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you know your situation, that you're a putugena, you know that nothing sort of supramundane, so to speak, can come out of this discussion with another putugena, but you do it just to highlight your ignorance further, which means you did begin with that self-honest attitude.

204
00:17:00,650 --> 00:17:03,291
[SPEAKER_01]: But in that same sense, you don't even need the other person.

205
00:17:03,311 --> 00:17:09,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Just pick up any philosophy book and look at like, okay, this is supposedly the wrong view.

206
00:17:10,945 --> 00:17:12,605
[SPEAKER_01]: But, have I understood it?

207
00:17:12,685 --> 00:17:12,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

208
00:17:13,085 --> 00:17:13,946
[SPEAKER_01]: And to what extent?

209
00:17:14,046 --> 00:17:15,326
[SPEAKER_01]: And where exactly it's wrong?

210
00:17:16,126 --> 00:17:16,926
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by then...

211
00:17:16,966 --> 00:17:18,186
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not looking for faults.

212
00:17:18,827 --> 00:17:23,968
[SPEAKER_01]: You're genuinely adopting that view in order to be able to understand the extent of it.

213
00:17:25,548 --> 00:17:29,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so... That's why often the Buddha would describe all these wrong views that were present at the time.

214
00:17:31,169 --> 00:17:35,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by fully understanding their extent, one would be much closer to the right view.

215
00:17:38,002 --> 00:17:43,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, discussing with another equally intelligent, equally self-transparent person, that can help.

216
00:17:48,170 --> 00:17:48,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop making noise.

217
00:17:50,672 --> 00:17:51,252
[SPEAKER_01]: That can help.

218
00:17:51,732 --> 00:17:55,035
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, reading other books or other teachings can help.

219
00:17:56,596 --> 00:18:01,520
[SPEAKER_01]: But not if you don't have that necessary self-transparent basis.

220
00:18:02,921 --> 00:18:04,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Come here.

221
00:18:05,403 --> 00:18:05,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Come here.

222
00:18:16,654 --> 00:18:22,720
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ideally, you want to be discussing these things with a sotāpanna, someone who has...

223
00:18:22,760 --> 00:18:23,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

224
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:24,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

225
00:18:24,461 --> 00:18:26,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, then you can... yeah, yeah.

226
00:18:26,063 --> 00:18:28,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, I think it is just stabbing in the dark.

227
00:18:28,805 --> 00:18:32,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you actually do...

228
00:18:33,790 --> 00:18:36,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you do actually stab something, you wouldn't know it.

229
00:18:37,652 --> 00:18:38,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

230
00:18:38,993 --> 00:18:43,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, hey, you can profit from it if you're already very self-transparent.

231
00:18:44,596 --> 00:18:50,759
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you would profit from reading other views, other things, because you do have self-transparency.

232
00:18:51,039 --> 00:18:59,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not looking for faults, you're not covering it up, you haven't just adopted the belief that this is wrong, but you're genuinely understanding the extent of it, and why it's wrong, and how it's wrong.

233
00:19:01,524 --> 00:19:06,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for a person who wants to discuss with a sotāpanna,

234
00:19:08,514 --> 00:19:09,475
[SPEAKER_00]: How can he find one?

235
00:19:09,735 --> 00:19:10,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

236
00:19:10,335 --> 00:19:11,235
[SPEAKER_01]: He has no reference.

237
00:19:11,855 --> 00:19:13,056
[SPEAKER_00]: What is his criteria?

238
00:19:13,496 --> 00:19:15,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this is what his criteria is.

239
00:19:15,617 --> 00:19:27,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the closest to the right criteria he can get is this self-transparency, being honest with oneself, and not being dismissive of other views, even if they are not pleasing to you.

240
00:19:27,822 --> 00:19:35,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, usually, people's criteria for their views, opinions, ideologies, beliefs, is basically emotional.

241
00:19:36,913 --> 00:19:39,554
[SPEAKER_01]: This is pleasing to me for whatever.

242
00:19:39,574 --> 00:19:48,419
[SPEAKER_01]: There is maybe a small kind of degree of reasoning involved, but mostly it's just this is emotionally pleasing to me.

243
00:19:48,459 --> 00:19:51,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, this is my view and that's why I'm defending it.

244
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:53,921
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I'm criticizing other views.

245
00:19:54,742 --> 00:19:57,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I have understood my view or I've understood other views.

246
00:19:57,383 --> 00:19:57,483
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

247
00:19:57,523 --> 00:20:01,765
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because, circumstantially, I adopted it and now I'm defending it at all cost.

248
00:20:02,620 --> 00:20:07,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that attitude is basically the main impediment.

249
00:20:07,283 --> 00:20:11,145
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the wrong attention.

250
00:20:11,205 --> 00:20:15,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that looking for faults in a speaker will prevent you understanding what he means.

251
00:20:16,275 --> 00:20:30,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And by not getting the meaning, then you might actually, sort of, cross over a sotāpanna, or even an arahant, who's been speaking, you've been listening to it, but you were not paying proper attention, as in you were dismissing it, or not listening to it carefully enough, and that's it.

252
00:20:30,905 --> 00:20:31,225
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gone.

253
00:20:31,485 --> 00:20:34,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And your views, your wrong views, your wrong criterias remained unaffected.

254
00:20:35,848 --> 00:20:37,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're none the wiser.

255
00:20:37,469 --> 00:20:42,092
[SPEAKER_01]: None the wiser even by, like, having a Buddha himself in front of you, teaching you.

256
00:20:44,737 --> 00:20:45,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

257
00:20:45,257 --> 00:21:00,384
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're self-transparent, honest with yourself, so you know your situation, you know that you're not free from suffering, not free from sensuality or greed or aversion, and then you also recognize the inevitability of having to have some views.

258
00:21:01,575 --> 00:21:07,596
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't judge... you don't judge your situation through your views, you judge your views through a situation.

259
00:21:08,336 --> 00:21:16,098
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you can't help having views, but at the same time you recognize, well, I'm not free from suffering, I'm not free from greed, aversion, delusion, I'm not a sotāpanna, basically.

260
00:21:16,578 --> 00:21:21,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, the views that I do hold and regard dear, I should put in question.

261
00:21:21,739 --> 00:21:28,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's because of those very views, that I'm not free from suffering, free from greed, aversion, delusion, I'm not a sotāpanna yet.

262
00:21:30,735 --> 00:21:37,540
[SPEAKER_01]: But people might think one thing, then they might think about the second thing of their views, but they'll never put it together and see that that's a contradiction.

263
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:42,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't have puthujjana's views that are right and not be a sotāpanna.

264
00:21:44,945 --> 00:21:46,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Because right view means sotāpatti.

265
00:21:46,867 --> 00:21:51,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't have the right view, you can't then entertain the notion that you're puttu...

266
00:21:51,650 --> 00:21:57,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, if you're not a puthujjana, if you're not a sotāpanna, if you're a puthujjana, you can't entertain a notion that you also have right views.

267
00:22:06,247 --> 00:22:11,992
[SPEAKER_01]: And that attitude is unpleasant, because it's like living in perpetual doubt of your own existence.

268
00:22:12,072 --> 00:22:24,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's very... can be very anxious, very... yeah, full... like lot... quite... lots of... lots of sort of concern, because any single view that then you... you look into, you realize, well, this might be wrong.

269
00:22:25,403 --> 00:22:29,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Then any confidence that you might have had as a Bhutujana gets completely undone, demolished.

270
00:22:30,327 --> 00:22:31,228
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the whole point.

271
00:22:33,173 --> 00:22:39,897
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why when the practice of dhamma, when thinking and reflecting upon views, it's not optional anymore.

272
00:22:40,098 --> 00:22:41,278
[SPEAKER_01]: It's existential, basically.

273
00:22:42,980 --> 00:22:44,060
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to do it.

274
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:47,923
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to clarify it, because you realize the peril of it.

275
00:22:49,404 --> 00:23:01,572
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just simply adopt a belief that... Well, you can adopt a belief, but if you are self-honest, if you do have that honesty, you wouldn't be able to.

276
00:23:03,068 --> 00:23:10,310
[SPEAKER_01]: As appealing as a belief might be, you wouldn't be able to, eh... Well, you would be able to at the expense of honest, self-honesty.

277
00:23:11,531 --> 00:23:22,054
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize that self-honesty should not be compromised ever, then, eh... that's your safeguard against adopting and proliferating things just simply upon a belief.

278
00:23:24,275 --> 00:23:27,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if you adopt something, it would be after heavy scrutiny.

279
00:23:29,356 --> 00:23:31,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And even then, you wouldn't adopt it as a belief.

280
00:23:32,792 --> 00:23:39,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You would adopt it pretty much as a strategy towards your practice of freedom from suffering.

281
00:23:40,115 --> 00:23:52,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if that strategy is not bearing any fruit, and you're not free from suffering, you're going to be upgrading it or changing it, and you won't be afraid to do it.

282
00:23:54,562 --> 00:23:58,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And faith... Well, that's... See, you can have...

283
00:24:00,383 --> 00:24:03,486
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have a belief without faith, but you can have faith without a belief.

284
00:24:04,606 --> 00:24:07,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the faith that we are talking about is the... is the...

285
00:24:07,829 --> 00:24:10,871
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's in the sutras it's mentioned, and I might have spoken about it before.

286
00:24:11,512 --> 00:24:15,956
[SPEAKER_01]: The faith we are talking about is the faith that you would have, that a person would have in a doctor.

287
00:24:17,557 --> 00:24:23,341
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you recognize your situation through that self-honest scrutiny of yourself, basically.

288
00:24:24,142 --> 00:24:28,926
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize that you need help, as in, you are a subject to suffering, you are prey to suffering,

289
00:24:29,327 --> 00:24:31,249
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know the way out from a reasoned suffering.

290
00:24:33,011 --> 00:24:35,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you find something that promises a way out.

291
00:24:35,153 --> 00:24:36,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have faith that it might work.

292
00:24:38,135 --> 00:24:39,397
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's applied, obviously.

293
00:24:40,278 --> 00:24:54,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, you recognize you have an illness, you go to a doctor, so you have faith that he can help you, that he can prescribe you medicine, and whatever, a course of treatment or something, that will then result in that freedom.

294
00:24:54,827 --> 00:24:56,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, from that ailment.

295
00:24:57,168 --> 00:25:06,754
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do start taking medicine and doing everything rightly, you understood doctor's instructions properly, and you're not being free from your ailment, well, maybe

296
00:25:07,414 --> 00:25:10,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith a bit longer, a bit more, keep applying it.

297
00:25:10,417 --> 00:25:17,383
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if it's nothing, there's no results, well, then you won't have a problem changing it, looking for a different way, because it's not working.

298
00:25:17,423 --> 00:25:22,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't done it out of a sort of emotional attitude, or you... nothing.

299
00:25:22,307 --> 00:25:25,310
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you really... you genuinely tried, and it didn't free you.

300
00:25:25,370 --> 00:25:33,817
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it does free you, then that faith disappears, because you have... you don't have to have faith that it will work, because now you know that it worked, and you are cured.

301
00:25:36,489 --> 00:25:44,015
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why in the suttas, like for Sotāpanna, his faith in the Buddha, dhamma, saṅgha has been replaced by the confirmed confidence.

302
00:25:44,035 --> 00:25:45,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was confirmed.

303
00:25:45,056 --> 00:25:50,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Now he can see, he can see his health as a direct result of having faith in this application.

304
00:25:52,521 --> 00:25:57,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, you need to be honest, because if you're not, you're going to be blaming a doctor.

305
00:25:59,527 --> 00:26:01,548
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to be blaming the one who gave the instruction.

306
00:26:03,890 --> 00:26:05,912
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, you first need to start with yourself.

307
00:26:10,705 --> 00:26:18,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it's not working, if you don't have patience, if it's not working quickly... Well, if you don't have patience and you're not self-transparent, you won't see that you're impatient.

308
00:26:19,935 --> 00:26:27,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you won't see that it's actually your lack of patience that's a problem, as in you need to take the medicine a bit longer.

309
00:26:28,185 --> 00:26:31,909
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you're just going to dismiss the doctor, who could have been Buddha himself.

310
00:26:34,539 --> 00:26:42,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I insist always on, like, self-transparency, because if you're not, you will be pointing fingers, basically, when there are no results to show for.

311
00:27:34,395 --> 00:27:42,142
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the instructions given by the Buddha... What are they?

312
00:27:42,282 --> 00:27:44,124
[SPEAKER_00]: What was his first instructions?

313
00:27:45,926 --> 00:27:50,690
[SPEAKER_00]: What was his first thing to do?

314
00:27:51,211 --> 00:27:52,772
[SPEAKER_01]: There's suffering, there's freedom from it.

315
00:27:53,693 --> 00:27:57,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have to... By the way, are you recording?

316
00:27:57,497 --> 00:27:57,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

317
00:28:07,247 --> 00:28:15,213
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha only gives precepts much later on in his... during his life, much later on.

318
00:28:15,573 --> 00:28:30,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he defines them specifically later on, but the the actual virtue that was sort of contained in the eight precepts has been praised by him throughout the suttas, in Digha, Inca, Majjhima, Inca.

319
00:28:30,805 --> 00:28:42,413
[SPEAKER_01]: He would say that the wise men of the past were virtuous, and so he was just... he was taking for granted, basically, that somebody who has already came to that point of practicing the dhamma has already had their virtue developed.

320
00:28:43,374 --> 00:28:47,497
[SPEAKER_01]: They've been restrained, moral, virtuous, honest, and so on.

321
00:28:47,537 --> 00:28:50,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Where did they get those... that morality from?

322
00:28:50,419 --> 00:28:50,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Who?

323
00:28:51,259 --> 00:28:51,980
[SPEAKER_00]: The people who...

324
00:28:52,340 --> 00:28:52,840
[SPEAKER_01]: In the past?

325
00:28:52,920 --> 00:28:53,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

326
00:28:53,421 --> 00:28:59,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, from understanding the natural principles, as in the universal principles of a human existence.

327
00:29:14,737 --> 00:29:15,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

328
00:29:15,097 --> 00:29:20,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding principles... Yeah.

329
00:29:20,420 --> 00:29:29,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, if you... Basically, if you understand why is virtue virtues, it's already the beginning of wisdom.

330
00:29:30,727 --> 00:29:36,390
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can also arrive at that understanding by taking virtue and faith, the faith we discussed.

331
00:29:37,170 --> 00:29:39,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have faith that virtue is necessary.

332
00:29:39,172 --> 00:29:42,734
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the doctor says, tells you, well, this medicine will work,

333
00:29:43,599 --> 00:29:45,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Only if you follow a strict diet.

334
00:29:46,320 --> 00:29:52,663
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're careless with what you eat, it's going to cancel the effectiveness of a prescription I'm giving you.

335
00:29:54,204 --> 00:29:56,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, some people will not have even that little bit of faith.

336
00:29:56,845 --> 00:30:12,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they do investigate, reflect upon themselves in that same transparent manner, well, you would see basically that virtue is virtuous because it does not contain any existential discrepancy or contradiction.

337
00:30:14,704 --> 00:30:18,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, in order to arrive at it, you are already developing wisdom.

338
00:30:20,209 --> 00:30:29,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, good things are good because they're basically aligned with the universal principles, with things that are most fundamental.

339
00:30:30,017 --> 00:30:30,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, for example,

340
00:30:31,932 --> 00:30:34,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Sharing and giving and charity is good.

341
00:30:34,435 --> 00:30:34,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why?

342
00:30:35,336 --> 00:30:36,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, people say, oh, it makes me feel good.

343
00:30:36,918 --> 00:30:37,819
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all secondary.

344
00:30:38,440 --> 00:30:48,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Existentially, charity is good, because it goes along the way of you not being owner of the things in the first place.

345
00:30:50,452 --> 00:30:53,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, everybody starts with the sense of ownership.

346
00:30:53,354 --> 00:30:55,835
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely gratuitous, but everybody owns.

347
00:30:55,916 --> 00:31:00,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody immediately feels they're the owner of their body, their senses.

348
00:31:00,599 --> 00:31:07,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, through engagement with the world, through the tools, which are your senses, which is your body, you start... you're fully identified with the tools.

349
00:31:09,084 --> 00:31:13,607
[SPEAKER_01]: With eye, nose, ears, and mouth, body, and everything else, basically.

350
00:31:14,971 --> 00:31:18,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't know why, but they said giving is good.

351
00:31:18,532 --> 00:31:29,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, giving is good, because you basically deprive yourself of something to give to somebody else, which resembles the experience of non-ownership of that which has been owned.

352
00:31:30,097 --> 00:31:33,098
[SPEAKER_01]: That which I still regard as mine, I give to somebody else.

353
00:31:34,858 --> 00:31:40,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can arrive at it either through doing that, and then contemplating it, or not doing it, but then you need to have discernment.

354
00:31:40,621 --> 00:31:44,602
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's highly unlikely you're going to arrive at it through stinginess and, eh...

355
00:31:46,065 --> 00:31:53,349
[SPEAKER_01]: And complete sort of lack of any virtue, basis of virtue, or charity and kindness.

356
00:31:56,770 --> 00:32:13,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can take any other thing that's regarded as good and virtuous, genuinely, you would see that it would be along the... it would be mirroring, it would be reflecting upon some principles of your existence, that you're living right there and then, but you're not aware of.

357
00:32:15,488 --> 00:32:17,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Lying, basically, it's another discrepancy.

358
00:32:18,290 --> 00:32:19,310
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a truth.

359
00:32:19,350 --> 00:32:20,571
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to override it.

360
00:32:22,032 --> 00:32:27,375
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to act upon that discrepancy and then perpetuate it as a non-discrepancy.

361
00:32:28,616 --> 00:32:29,677
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's unwholesome.

362
00:32:30,057 --> 00:32:30,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Same stealing.

363
00:32:32,018 --> 00:32:35,881
[SPEAKER_01]: It reinforces that perverted pattern of human existence.

364
00:32:35,981 --> 00:32:40,263
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I am the owner of my body and my senses and things that I can gain through.

365
00:32:40,323 --> 00:32:44,286
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point of then taking that which you perceive through your senses as not yours.

366
00:32:45,289 --> 00:32:48,431
[SPEAKER_01]: As an extension of the very attitude that you have towards your own senses.

367
00:32:51,592 --> 00:32:53,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Because basically, you do become a thief.

368
00:32:53,954 --> 00:32:56,415
[SPEAKER_01]: We might have all started as... as borrowing.

369
00:32:57,075 --> 00:33:02,238
[SPEAKER_01]: We're all just... just... just basically renting this body and these senses, so to speak.

370
00:33:02,698 --> 00:33:04,079
[SPEAKER_01]: They're given to us for our use.

371
00:33:04,639 --> 00:33:10,062
[SPEAKER_01]: But the moment you start regarding as yours, that which you have rented, you have effectively stolen.

372
00:33:14,084 --> 00:33:14,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Taken from Mara.

373
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:16,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

374
00:33:16,581 --> 00:33:17,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the Buddha did say that.

375
00:33:17,582 --> 00:33:31,837
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, because people take and use that which doesn't belong to them, well, they take it as theirs, that which doesn't belong to them, that's why the Mahārāja controls them and does what he wants and that's why they're subject to suffering and the whole...

376
00:33:33,934 --> 00:33:35,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Death and misery and everything else.

377
00:33:35,495 --> 00:33:38,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the monks asked, what are those things?

378
00:33:38,458 --> 00:33:47,185
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, it's eyes and objects, ears and sounds, nose and smells, mouth, sorry, tongue and tastes, body and touches.

379
00:33:48,887 --> 00:33:51,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the things, basically, that don't belong to you.

380
00:33:51,549 --> 00:33:52,370
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just renting.

381
00:33:52,510 --> 00:33:53,251
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just borrowed.

382
00:33:53,891 --> 00:33:55,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Borrowed jewelry, as the Buddha would refer to it.

383
00:33:56,493 --> 00:34:00,137
[SPEAKER_01]: But you start entertaining the notion that you are their owner, they are their own.

384
00:34:02,523 --> 00:34:09,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not like a metaphysical secret that you need to figure out in order to drop it, to not regard it as yours.

385
00:34:09,351 --> 00:34:11,393
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you just need to dwell on the fact that they're not yours.

386
00:34:12,755 --> 00:34:21,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Just need to keep really, really, really investigating the meaning of not mine and then applying it to yourself.

387
00:34:24,353 --> 00:34:33,905
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, obviously, in the most general terms, that's what the Buddha would describe, like, well, if you were the owner of your body, for example, would that body be subjected to sickness, aging and death?

388
00:34:34,265 --> 00:34:34,666
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't.

389
00:34:35,447 --> 00:34:38,370
[SPEAKER_01]: See, many people think that, oh, yeah, of course, I understand that, I'm subject to all these things.

390
00:34:38,751 --> 00:34:43,737
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you truly understood... if you were to truly understand that, you would be completely free from appropriation.

391
00:34:44,788 --> 00:34:45,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

392
00:34:45,548 --> 00:34:47,789
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're self-transparent, then you would see a discrepancy.

393
00:34:48,429 --> 00:34:50,290
[SPEAKER_01]: On one hand, I'm thinking I understood it.

394
00:34:51,090 --> 00:34:55,892
[SPEAKER_01]: On another hand, I don't have that which is supposed to be the result of that understanding.

395
00:34:56,052 --> 00:35:00,054
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, freedom from appropriation, freedom from suffering, freedom from greed, aversion, delusion.

396
00:35:01,394 --> 00:35:06,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you see that discrepancy, that contradiction, then you'll know exactly where to do the work.

397
00:35:06,916 --> 00:35:08,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, okay, I need to understand this further.

398
00:35:09,277 --> 00:35:11,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And what I think understanding is, it's not sufficient.

399
00:35:13,249 --> 00:35:15,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Or sometimes it's just plain-out wrong.

400
00:35:16,011 --> 00:35:23,034
[SPEAKER_01]: But you haven't... you wouldn't have seen it unless you brought this discrepancy, like, to the fore and fully understood it.

401
00:35:25,156 --> 00:35:38,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, one takes on virtue given from an external...

402
00:35:39,953 --> 00:35:40,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, exactly.

403
00:35:41,033 --> 00:35:42,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Set of values, or whatever.

404
00:35:43,194 --> 00:35:55,518
[SPEAKER_01]: But you could see that in every society there is a degree of it, because it would kind of seep down into, like, you know, collective society awareness, that's sort of among the, like, completely unenlightened people, but you still get the sense of it.

405
00:35:56,078 --> 00:35:58,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Sharing is good, patience is good, all these things.

406
00:35:59,699 --> 00:36:02,800
[SPEAKER_01]: It is good, but it's also good to understand why these things are good.

407
00:36:05,661 --> 00:36:07,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then you can take it even further.

408
00:36:10,652 --> 00:36:14,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah, you can discover virtue by yourself.

409
00:36:16,056 --> 00:36:16,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I suppose.

410
00:36:16,796 --> 00:36:17,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddha did.

411
00:36:17,857 --> 00:36:20,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you could say that the Buddha did discover it by himself.

412
00:36:21,039 --> 00:36:26,684
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's highly unlikely, because you would kind of need some pointers, some external reference point.

413
00:36:26,704 --> 00:36:31,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You would go a bit deeper without having... Well, you can only take it so far, basically.

414
00:36:31,787 --> 00:36:39,533
[SPEAKER_01]: You could discover virtue by yourself, to a degree, but not fully understand it to the point of becoming a sotāpanna.

415
00:36:40,243 --> 00:36:46,448
[SPEAKER_01]: As in the one who has understood sīla, and thus he's free from sīla-bhāta-paramāsa, which is virtue and duty.

416
00:36:46,488 --> 00:36:48,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Fetter of virtue and duty he's free from.

417
00:36:49,390 --> 00:36:50,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's another thing.

418
00:36:51,492 --> 00:36:53,653
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to understand virtue, you need to have it.

419
00:36:54,354 --> 00:36:57,716
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be a non-virtuous person and then understand the nature of virtue.

420
00:36:58,457 --> 00:37:01,579
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be having it present in your presently arisen experience.

421
00:37:01,839 --> 00:37:03,761
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you need to be virtuous, basically.

422
00:37:05,307 --> 00:37:09,669
[SPEAKER_01]: But being virtues in itself, it's nice, but it does not result in wisdom.

423
00:37:09,769 --> 00:37:11,349
[SPEAKER_01]: And if there's no wisdom, there's no liberation.

424
00:37:12,790 --> 00:37:15,691
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, obviously, sīla and virtue, it's not the dhamma.

425
00:37:16,752 --> 00:37:20,173
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why also dhamma is impossible to arise without sīla.

426
00:37:20,493 --> 00:37:24,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how can you understand... how can you develop understanding of sīla without sīla?

427
00:37:25,335 --> 00:37:25,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

428
00:37:27,196 --> 00:37:32,258
[SPEAKER_01]: But many people don't see that connection, which is why that is the very first fetter of sīla bhātta-paramāsa.

429
00:37:33,437 --> 00:37:36,679
[SPEAKER_01]: People think you must be virtuous, and yes, you do.

430
00:37:36,959 --> 00:37:38,040
[SPEAKER_01]: You do have to be virtuous.

431
00:37:38,520 --> 00:37:43,944
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they think, okay, so I'm doing that, and now the practice of dhamma and understanding is over here.

432
00:37:45,024 --> 00:37:50,027
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm doing my meditation technique, I'm reading the suttas, I'm studying Pali, and I'm virtuous.

433
00:37:50,067 --> 00:37:51,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's all contributing.

434
00:37:51,428 --> 00:37:51,568
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

435
00:37:52,789 --> 00:37:57,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you have the virtue, you have the morality, you have the restraint.

436
00:37:58,378 --> 00:38:06,402
[SPEAKER_01]: You take it then further and practice, like, the sensory strength even of things that are not necessarily non-virtuous, but are not virtuous enough, perhaps.

437
00:38:07,463 --> 00:38:10,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Such as pursuing sight, sound, smell, taste, touches that are distracting.

438
00:38:10,905 --> 00:38:16,568
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not necessarily rooted in anger and breaking of moral precepts or rooted in sensuality.

439
00:38:17,203 --> 00:38:18,924
[SPEAKER_01]: And breaking celibacy, precepts and so on.

440
00:38:19,004 --> 00:38:21,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But they can be rooted in distraction and carelessness.

441
00:38:21,586 --> 00:38:22,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop doing that.

442
00:38:22,867 --> 00:38:25,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you understand that very basis.

443
00:38:25,448 --> 00:38:27,389
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to go elsewhere.

444
00:38:27,429 --> 00:38:33,513
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need some other, like, whatever, developing of some sort of, ah, sapra mundane concentration.

445
00:38:33,693 --> 00:38:33,813
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

446
00:38:33,933 --> 00:38:35,274
[SPEAKER_01]: You already have it in front of you.

447
00:38:35,735 --> 00:38:42,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you need to start making the effort to cut through that fog of ignorance in regard to it.

448
00:38:43,099 --> 00:38:44,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't just take it for granted that it's good.

449
00:38:45,380 --> 00:38:46,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand it.

450
00:38:46,441 --> 00:38:47,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Why it is good.

451
00:38:47,701 --> 00:38:52,984
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the definition of sotāpanna is he's the one who knows good as good and bad as bad.

452
00:38:53,684 --> 00:38:56,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana is like a virtuous putujana.

453
00:38:56,106 --> 00:39:01,228
[SPEAKER_01]: A good putujana is the one who doesn't know good as good, bad as bad.

454
00:39:02,649 --> 00:39:10,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do want to practice meditation and contemplation, practice it within the basis of your virtuous behavior and sensory strength.

455
00:39:11,521 --> 00:39:13,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Not left, right, before or after it.

456
00:39:14,382 --> 00:39:14,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Within it.

457
00:39:15,382 --> 00:39:17,403
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, when this, this is.

458
00:39:18,283 --> 00:39:22,585
[SPEAKER_01]: When the basis of virtue is present, me trying to understand it is also present.

459
00:39:23,905 --> 00:39:39,630
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is to understand that they're not found elsewhere or they're not given to you through some sort of divine providence, through some sort of super concentration you developed and then you have this great revelation.

460
00:39:40,546 --> 00:39:43,146
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop it on the basis that you maintain.

461
00:39:43,807 --> 00:39:48,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're maintaining the basis of virtue, and then you develop your understanding of it.

462
00:39:49,628 --> 00:39:49,968
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

463
00:39:51,328 --> 00:40:04,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why for Sotāpanna, who has understood the nature of virtue, who has understood good is good, bad is bad, all he needs to do is continue practicing nekama-saṅkhapa, as in the video we described it.

464
00:40:04,710 --> 00:40:06,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts of renunciation, basically.

465
00:40:06,611 --> 00:40:08,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being virtuous and sense restraint.

466
00:40:09,537 --> 00:40:18,826
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through doing it, he's developing that very understanding of it.

467
00:40:23,851 --> 00:40:29,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you would also, as the Buddha would often encourage people, to see the danger in the slightest fault.

468
00:40:30,198 --> 00:40:32,440
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you would start seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

469
00:40:33,415 --> 00:40:34,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it's just a small thing.

470
00:40:34,376 --> 00:40:34,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

471
00:40:35,116 --> 00:40:36,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Big thing or small thing.

472
00:40:36,337 --> 00:40:38,919
[SPEAKER_01]: If it partakes in the nature of unwholesome, it's unwholesome.

473
00:40:38,959 --> 00:40:43,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's directly against the principle of dhamma, the universal principles.

474
00:40:50,107 --> 00:40:59,334
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it would be enough for somebody to receive instruction as simple as avoid anything that's unwholesome and cultivate anything that's wholesome.

475
00:41:04,415 --> 00:41:17,005
[SPEAKER_01]: As they did in the suttas, the the Buddha's foster mother, when she asked for that instruction, and the Buddha said, like, anything you know, it's unwholesome, abandon it, and anything you know, it's wholesome, cultivate.

476
00:41:17,385 --> 00:41:21,388
[SPEAKER_01]: And then she went for, like, seclusion for three months and so on, and came back, an arahant.

477
00:41:24,591 --> 00:41:25,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully enlightened.

478
00:41:27,213 --> 00:41:28,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully freed, basically.

479
00:41:28,754 --> 00:41:29,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Because she fully understood it.

480
00:41:32,202 --> 00:41:36,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see how many people today... oh, yeah, of course, this is bad, this is good.

481
00:41:38,584 --> 00:41:40,984
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to truly understand that, you'd be at least Sotāpanna.

482
00:41:41,004 --> 00:41:47,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if you think you have a knowledge of what's good, what's bad, ask yourself, are you Sotāpanna?

483
00:41:47,206 --> 00:41:47,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you a stream-enterer?

484
00:41:48,747 --> 00:41:54,208
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer is no, then don't take that knowledge for granted, because it's not sufficient.

485
00:41:54,268 --> 00:41:56,869
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it might be good and sufficient for mundane terms,

486
00:41:59,400 --> 00:42:02,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's... well, exactly.

487
00:42:02,461 --> 00:42:08,443
[SPEAKER_01]: If you might be, means you're not free from another fetter, which is in that first group, which is fetter of doubt.

488
00:42:08,904 --> 00:42:10,844
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know you're a sotāpanna, means you're not sotāpanna.

489
00:42:14,826 --> 00:42:18,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding what sotāpanna is, makes you one.

490
00:42:21,849 --> 00:42:24,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding what sīlabhātparamāsa is, frees you from it.

491
00:42:25,610 --> 00:42:26,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding what a self-view is,

492
00:42:28,325 --> 00:42:28,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It frees you from it.

493
00:42:30,506 --> 00:42:32,847
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say, I might be free from self-view, but I don't know it yet.

494
00:42:33,307 --> 00:42:43,811
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you're not.

495
00:42:46,972 --> 00:42:52,294
[SPEAKER_01]: I mentioned yesterday, when I was talking to those people, eh... with...

496
00:42:52,314 --> 00:42:53,554
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to mention names, eh...

497
00:42:56,482 --> 00:43:09,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this whole, like, self-reflexive attitude is, like, you really want to do it in order to gain wisdom, to learn from it, basically.

498
00:43:09,386 --> 00:43:16,367
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be... You're not doing it because you want to justify your actions.

499
00:43:16,427 --> 00:43:18,468
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's how people usually be doing it.

500
00:43:18,748 --> 00:43:21,269
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not gonna say it again.

501
00:43:23,389 --> 00:43:23,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Ciao.

502
00:43:28,638 --> 00:43:45,002
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you really want to ask yourself, basically, like, say, when it comes to practice of dhamma, why are you doing it?

503
00:43:47,123 --> 00:43:48,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, really clarify.

504
00:43:50,344 --> 00:43:52,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Where do you want to arrive with it?

505
00:43:52,485 --> 00:43:53,105
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the goal?

506
00:43:56,438 --> 00:44:01,982
[SPEAKER_01]: And just... or in general, in any areas of your life, basically, just ask yourself, what do I want to do with my life?

507
00:44:02,042 --> 00:44:02,883
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my purpose?

508
00:44:05,965 --> 00:44:11,149
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't ask and expect immediate answer or something like that, but ask and then think about it.

509
00:44:12,510 --> 00:44:15,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if the answer presents itself, well, then question that answer.

510
00:44:16,133 --> 00:44:18,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most people would be...

511
00:44:20,027 --> 00:44:22,549
[SPEAKER_01]: We'd be going through life, basically, like...

512
00:44:22,569 --> 00:44:26,853
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if there's an English expression, but certainly back in Serbia, like a headless fly.

513
00:44:28,314 --> 00:44:28,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Headless chicken.

514
00:44:29,455 --> 00:44:34,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know, like a fly that doesn't have... and it's just flying like mad, left and right, hitting into windows.

515
00:44:34,639 --> 00:44:38,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still flying, but it doesn't have the head, so it doesn't see where anymore.

516
00:44:38,923 --> 00:44:47,850
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people would actually be able to help themselves if they just stop for a second and ask yourself, like, why am I doing this particular thing?

517
00:44:47,930 --> 00:44:48,351
[SPEAKER_01]: What for?

518
00:44:49,237 --> 00:44:49,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I want it?

519
00:44:49,998 --> 00:44:50,678
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I want?

520
00:44:51,679 --> 00:44:52,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people don't even know what they want.

521
00:44:54,901 --> 00:45:01,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just like habitually scratching the itch, like an animal, basically, and going through things as they arise.

522
00:45:04,053 --> 00:45:11,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the attitude would help even in terms of like an ordinary lay life, somebody who's not interested in greater development of the dhamma or so on.

523
00:45:11,899 --> 00:45:14,160
[SPEAKER_01]: But let alone for somebody who does want to practice dhamma.

524
00:45:14,361 --> 00:45:15,521
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just completely necessary.

525
00:45:16,042 --> 00:45:24,568
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to develop that self-reflection, that transparency and that kind of scrutiny of your own self, of your own thoughts, of your own intentions.

526
00:45:28,031 --> 00:45:31,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it will force the correct perspective upon you.

527
00:45:31,509 --> 00:45:33,330
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have it willingly.

528
00:45:33,370 --> 00:45:35,812
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just willingly adopt the correct perspective.

529
00:45:37,334 --> 00:45:41,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any perspective you know of, it's already perspective that's affected by greed, aversion, delusion.

530
00:45:44,259 --> 00:45:47,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just ask yourself, where do I want to be?

531
00:45:47,882 --> 00:45:54,487
[SPEAKER_01]: And say, in one year time, or two years time, provided I live that long.

532
00:45:54,607 --> 00:45:55,348
[SPEAKER_01]: What's my purpose?

533
00:45:55,428 --> 00:45:56,048
[SPEAKER_01]: What's my goal?

534
00:45:57,560 --> 00:46:05,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, you can basically review things that you already got committed to or engaged, and it's just like, well, why am I doing this?

535
00:46:06,204 --> 00:46:08,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I still doing this?

536
00:46:08,325 --> 00:46:09,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did I start doing this?

537
00:46:11,027 --> 00:46:12,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And see where the answers come up.

538
00:46:14,148 --> 00:46:15,349
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be successful.

539
00:46:16,309 --> 00:46:17,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so what for?

540
00:46:19,592 --> 00:46:23,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, I'm doing this job because I want to earn money and be my own boss.

541
00:46:23,455 --> 00:46:26,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that a good thing, being your own boss?

542
00:46:26,977 --> 00:46:28,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that something you value?

543
00:46:31,320 --> 00:46:35,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the answer would be because it's unpleasant to have somebody else telling me what to do.

544
00:46:36,324 --> 00:46:45,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so why is then the unpleasant experience of having somebody else tell you what to do, why is that a problem for you on the personal level?

545
00:46:45,771 --> 00:46:48,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Why can't you just remain indifferent towards the equanimous?

546
00:46:49,552 --> 00:46:50,093
[SPEAKER_01]: And just do it.

547
00:46:50,613 --> 00:46:51,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it a problem?

548
00:46:52,014 --> 00:46:54,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a problem because it's making me suffer.

549
00:46:55,697 --> 00:46:56,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so suffering is a problem.

550
00:46:57,379 --> 00:47:05,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, you're doing this job, ten hours a day, earning loads of money, trying to study, just because you don't... so you don't suffer.

551
00:47:06,907 --> 00:47:11,711
[SPEAKER_01]: But, is there a guarantee that you won't suffer after all that effort you put in?

552
00:47:12,271 --> 00:47:12,712
[SPEAKER_01]: There isn't.

553
00:47:13,552 --> 00:47:14,293
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, secondly,

554
00:47:14,764 --> 00:47:20,885
[SPEAKER_01]: That all effort you put in, you might have overcome possibility of suffering on account of somebody else telling you what to do.

555
00:47:21,425 --> 00:47:24,546
[SPEAKER_01]: But, there're millions of other ways of suffering in your life.

556
00:47:25,366 --> 00:47:34,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you invested your... like, your whole life, how many years of building something up, just to address like 0.1% of possibilities of suffering.

557
00:47:35,688 --> 00:47:38,349
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't freed yourself from the nature of suffering.

558
00:47:39,269 --> 00:47:42,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You've only freed yourself from one particular manifestation of it.

559
00:47:43,674 --> 00:47:48,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Having somebody else, less qualified than me, telling me what to do and bossing me around.

560
00:47:51,181 --> 00:47:53,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that a... is that a fair investment?

561
00:47:53,443 --> 00:47:54,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that a good investment?

562
00:47:56,345 --> 00:47:57,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize it isn't.

563
00:47:57,867 --> 00:48:00,589
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, obviously, the inevitable question is, well, what do I do then?

564
00:48:01,570 --> 00:48:03,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you ask yourself, what's more important?

565
00:48:06,658 --> 00:48:09,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Being completely free from suffering, or partially.

566
00:48:09,966 --> 00:48:15,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can choose whatever you want, but choose... then you will choose it... choose it responsibly.

567
00:48:16,830 --> 00:48:21,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you know, okay, there is a possibility of being free from suffering altogether.

568
00:48:22,092 --> 00:48:24,012
[SPEAKER_01]: But I feel, it feels a bit daunting for me.

569
00:48:24,853 --> 00:48:28,654
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm kind of satisfied with sort of halfway.

570
00:48:29,594 --> 00:48:30,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's fine.

571
00:48:30,295 --> 00:48:32,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you will take responsibility for it.

572
00:48:32,495 --> 00:48:39,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, when the other half of suffering that you haven't freed yourself, comes your way, you won't be blaming others, you won't be resorting to ill will, eh...

573
00:48:40,201 --> 00:48:42,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Psychologizing your suffering, finding excuses for it.

574
00:48:42,883 --> 00:48:43,003
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

575
00:48:43,223 --> 00:48:49,248
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll accept it as part of the deal that you accepted when you clarified your perspective and your goals.

576
00:48:50,809 --> 00:49:02,698
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you haven't done that, everything will always be someone else's fault and you're not going to be responsible for anything you do, yet you're going to be equally bound, even more probably, with suffering and you won't be able to do anything about it.

577
00:49:04,019 --> 00:49:08,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means your efforts are completely misdirected, yet that was the goal from the start.

578
00:49:12,892 --> 00:49:16,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, for somebody who does practice dhamma, just, again, ask yourself, well, why am I practicing dhamma?

579
00:49:17,035 --> 00:49:17,795
[SPEAKER_01]: To be free from suffering.

580
00:49:18,116 --> 00:49:21,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, is my current way of practicing, is it contributing?

581
00:49:21,558 --> 00:49:24,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, I hope it is, but do you know for sure that it is?

582
00:49:25,721 --> 00:49:26,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it must.

583
00:49:26,322 --> 00:49:27,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's not the right answer.

584
00:49:27,963 --> 00:49:30,164
[SPEAKER_01]: It must be contributing, because I've been doing it for 20 years.

585
00:49:31,925 --> 00:49:34,988
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's contributing, it means your suffering is lessening.

586
00:49:35,228 --> 00:49:39,551
[SPEAKER_01]: It means possibility to suffer is being lessened, fading out.

587
00:49:41,023 --> 00:49:45,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to be honest and admit, because the answer might not be a pleasing one.

588
00:49:45,265 --> 00:49:51,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially if you invested 20 years in practice, and you still can't tell whether you're anything closer to being free from suffering.

589
00:49:52,849 --> 00:49:54,649
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that is an absolutely necessary step.

590
00:49:54,729 --> 00:50:04,554
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you ignore to address it in such a direct, non-compromising manner, you can be practicing for hundreds of years, and still remain a putu jhāna.

591
00:50:06,895 --> 00:50:10,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, all it would take for you to stop being puthujjana is that.

592
00:50:11,337 --> 00:50:12,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Just admit that you are puthujjana.

593
00:50:14,579 --> 00:50:18,321
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't... like... because some people might have actually done the right practice, but they don't know it.

594
00:50:19,002 --> 00:50:21,283
[SPEAKER_01]: But they can only find out once they admit that they don't know it.

595
00:50:22,443 --> 00:50:25,905
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if it were right, their efforts, their reflection, then they'd... oh, yeah.

596
00:50:27,406 --> 00:50:31,148
[SPEAKER_01]: But because they are too afraid to go that way, they'll never find out.

597
00:50:31,548 --> 00:50:33,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they won't be a sotāpanna.

598
00:50:35,110 --> 00:50:36,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's a great loss.

599
00:50:45,685 --> 00:50:52,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, all your efforts remain undermined just by lacking that last grain of courage.

600
00:50:54,152 --> 00:51:04,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Just admitting to yourself that you don't know, truly, on that existential level.

601
00:51:04,300 --> 00:51:08,624
[SPEAKER_00]: After so many years of practice, of so-called practice, of trying to...

602
00:51:15,209 --> 00:51:21,793
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose, like, someone trying to get rich and then pretending that they are already rich after ten years.

603
00:51:21,813 --> 00:51:22,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, pretty much, yeah.

604
00:51:22,413 --> 00:51:24,275
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, again, it's another discrepancy.

605
00:51:24,295 --> 00:51:24,795
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

606
00:51:24,815 --> 00:51:27,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, arahant is completely, hundred percent discrepancy-free.

607
00:51:27,957 --> 00:51:30,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Any discrepancy in the existential is impossible.

608
00:51:31,199 --> 00:51:33,520
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a definition of an arahant.

609
00:51:34,761 --> 00:51:36,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start recognizing that, you would see exactly, yeah.

610
00:51:36,983 --> 00:51:42,286
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm, I'm, I'm, I'm practicing, or I'll say, I'm, I'm, I'm, I'm working to be rich, but I'm not rich

611
00:51:44,031 --> 00:51:55,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can be blaming others, or I can accept others as a factor in me not being rich, and then incorporate it in my efforts, and then upgrade my efforts.

612
00:51:56,020 --> 00:51:59,582
[SPEAKER_01]: But only once I admit that I'm poor.

613
00:52:00,763 --> 00:52:12,592
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't admit it, it will be everybody else's fault, that's preventing you from getting there, or whatever, and you're still going to be doing the same things, because of which you're not getting where you want to get.

614
00:52:14,647 --> 00:52:23,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't be questioning your efforts of getting rich, acquiring wealth, despite your immediate poverty.

615
00:52:24,534 --> 00:52:33,160
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is there like a time period that one could say, you could try a certain... Well, that's what... we go back to the very first thing we were talking about, the faith.

616
00:52:33,541 --> 00:52:34,221
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah.

617
00:52:34,721 --> 00:52:37,603
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to have some faith, and you have to give it some time.

618
00:52:37,944 --> 00:52:42,267
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, after a while, you might even have to cut your losses and just restart.

619
00:52:44,434 --> 00:52:46,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not doing it for the sake of doing it.

620
00:52:46,776 --> 00:52:48,377
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it to be free from suffering.

621
00:52:48,397 --> 00:52:49,538
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it for the gold.

622
00:52:50,739 --> 00:52:51,760
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it for the fruit.

623
00:52:51,940 --> 00:52:54,883
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it to arrive at that degree of liberation.

624
00:53:00,347 --> 00:53:06,172
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, somewhere down the line, for some people, that kind of got pushed in the background.

625
00:53:06,752 --> 00:53:13,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, they became too precious, too protective of the efforts they invested in their practice.

626
00:53:14,357 --> 00:53:16,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Despite the lack of result.

627
00:53:17,720 --> 00:53:18,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they defend that.

628
00:53:18,861 --> 00:53:21,763
[SPEAKER_01]: They defend that kind of indefensible position, really.

629
00:53:21,903 --> 00:53:22,664
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a contradiction.

630
00:53:23,364 --> 00:53:29,289
[SPEAKER_01]: But they say, well, I might not be a sotāpanna, but surely all this effort could not have been wasted.

631
00:53:30,650 --> 00:53:31,911
[SPEAKER_01]: And they refuse to let go of it.

632
00:53:32,211 --> 00:53:32,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And then...

633
00:53:32,852 --> 00:53:34,093
[SPEAKER_01]: But that means they have to keep doing it.

634
00:53:34,668 --> 00:53:41,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they change their effort, that would be almost like the affirmation, the fact that they've been wasting... that they've been misguided and misdirecting their efforts.

635
00:53:41,670 --> 00:53:45,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, they have to stick to it, just to keep validating it for themselves that they are not wrong.

636
00:53:46,811 --> 00:53:50,892
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, they get becoming more and more wrong with each time.

637
00:53:50,912 --> 00:53:57,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that... that transparency, if it were there to begin with, got completely obscured.

638
00:54:09,943 --> 00:54:13,764
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, question yourself and be honest.

639
00:54:13,804 --> 00:54:15,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't fabricate, don't push for answers.

640
00:54:17,045 --> 00:54:17,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Just be honest.

641
00:54:18,426 --> 00:54:22,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, where am I at in my practice?

642
00:54:22,587 --> 00:54:23,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I complacent?

643
00:54:25,308 --> 00:54:26,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I think I have time?

644
00:54:27,068 --> 00:54:28,188
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, how long will I live?

645
00:54:29,289 --> 00:54:29,929
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things.

646
00:54:32,850 --> 00:54:36,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And you read the suttas, that's all the time you're talking about.

647
00:54:45,742 --> 00:54:50,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't do it for some sort of gaining some sort of emotional kind of uplifting.

648
00:54:50,623 --> 00:54:54,845
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're doing it for understanding.

649
00:54:54,865 --> 00:54:59,886
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it to develop clarity, to maintain perspective, basically.

650
00:54:59,926 --> 00:55:00,746
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're doing it.

651
00:55:02,647 --> 00:55:03,287
[SPEAKER_01]: And same again.

652
00:55:03,467 --> 00:55:06,248
[SPEAKER_01]: This is like from the point of view of practicing the Dhamma and the Buddha's teaching.

653
00:55:06,628 --> 00:55:14,890
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if you just want to live a wholesome, skillful life as a, you know, a normal lay person, not interested in some

654
00:55:15,347 --> 00:55:20,329
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, renouncing the world that has family and job, still, it's gonna help you.

655
00:55:21,489 --> 00:55:27,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It's certainly gonna strengthen you and develop your mind to deal with anything else that comes your way in the lay life.

656
00:55:27,211 --> 00:55:28,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And there are a lot of things coming people's way.

657
00:55:30,052 --> 00:55:31,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Lots of pressure, lots of stress, lots of burden.

658
00:55:33,919 --> 00:55:37,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Initial responses, you just deal by any means necessary.

659
00:55:37,960 --> 00:55:44,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start dealing it rightly, through the right perspective, restraint and virtue, it's going to make things more difficult in the beginning.

660
00:55:44,943 --> 00:55:48,544
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the long run, it just creates this resilience that nothing else can match.

661
00:55:50,165 --> 00:55:51,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of wisdom, basically.

662
00:55:53,386 --> 00:56:00,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you can have it to different degrees, but... Having perspective on yourself in a situation.

663
00:56:01,115 --> 00:56:04,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just being absorbed and pulled by the immediacy of a situation.

664
00:56:05,156 --> 00:56:09,658
[SPEAKER_01]: And which usually results in being quite reactionary.

665
00:56:10,738 --> 00:56:18,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Situations like this, I react like this, just like an animal.

666
00:56:19,381 --> 00:56:20,762
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's also useful before.

667
00:56:20,982 --> 00:56:21,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Like if people...

668
00:56:21,762 --> 00:56:24,063
[SPEAKER_01]: The sooner people start thinking like this, better.

669
00:56:24,223 --> 00:56:29,245
[SPEAKER_01]: As in thinking in terms of self-reflecting and really questioning themselves all the time.

670
00:56:30,407 --> 00:56:39,752
[SPEAKER_01]: The sooner the better, because you would have committed less in life, as in started a family and now you have people there to look after and so on, that you can't just turn your back on.

671
00:56:41,013 --> 00:56:49,918
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, it's not too late, because there is no end to a human proliferation in the world, to the proliferation of sensuality and the wrong view and lack of restraint.

672
00:56:49,998 --> 00:56:50,818
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no end to that.

673
00:56:52,159 --> 00:56:54,320
[SPEAKER_01]: The only end to that is self-reflection.

674
00:56:54,480 --> 00:56:58,002
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then prevent you from proliferating carelessly.

675
00:56:59,294 --> 00:57:00,535
[SPEAKER_01]: It's true protection, isn't it?

676
00:57:00,635 --> 00:57:01,036
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

677
00:57:01,096 --> 00:57:01,977
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't...

678
00:57:01,997 --> 00:57:06,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking up responsibility is a true protection.

679
00:57:06,402 --> 00:57:06,642
Yeah.

680
00:57:06,702 --> 00:57:07,323
For yourself.

681
00:57:20,114 --> 00:57:25,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting into a group or a certain nationality or identity... That's the opposite.

682
00:57:25,138 --> 00:57:25,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the opposite.

683
00:57:26,519 --> 00:57:30,582
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you have already disowned your responsibility.

684
00:57:31,082 --> 00:57:34,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you find affirmation in that act by identifying with a greater group.

685
00:57:35,546 --> 00:57:37,588
[SPEAKER_01]: That just obscures it even further.

686
00:57:38,508 --> 00:57:43,873
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you already covered up your personal responsibility for your personal existence and your personal choices.

687
00:57:45,174 --> 00:57:46,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's just intoxicating.

688
00:57:46,495 --> 00:57:47,736
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the masses, you know.

689
00:57:50,620 --> 00:57:58,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And even things that you wouldn't have necessarily done on a personal level, if you were with yourself, questioning yourself, you would have... you do it in a group.

690
00:58:00,427 --> 00:58:05,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, it just got all absorbed in this impersonal they, as Heidegger would have been talking about.

691
00:58:06,731 --> 00:58:10,233
[SPEAKER_01]: That now is just acting as this complete protection and you can do whatever you want.

692
00:58:10,253 --> 00:58:12,515
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like mob, definition of a mob mentality.

693
00:58:13,263 --> 00:58:18,589
[SPEAKER_01]: When you know these things are wrong and you wouldn't have done them if you were on your own or with somebody else who is not like...

694
00:58:19,270 --> 00:58:21,352
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, it's just... mob is intoxicated.

695
00:58:22,872 --> 00:58:23,892
[SPEAKER_01]: And why it's intoxicating?

696
00:58:23,912 --> 00:58:30,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just extension of complete lack of... well, of complete cover-up of your personal responsibility.

697
00:58:31,934 --> 00:58:35,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you just do stuff that you were not supposed to for their very sake.

698
00:58:35,594 --> 00:58:37,155
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be quite inviting to do it.

699
00:58:37,835 --> 00:58:39,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, let's smash these windows and break this.

700
00:58:39,855 --> 00:58:43,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they've done anything to us or I have any gain.

701
00:58:43,176 --> 00:58:43,316
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

702
00:58:43,736 --> 00:58:45,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because you're not supposed to do it.

703
00:58:45,576 --> 00:58:47,076
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do it, you'll be responsible for it.

704
00:58:47,136 --> 00:58:48,076
[SPEAKER_01]: But now we're in a mob.

705
00:58:48,597 --> 00:58:49,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Who cares?

706
00:58:49,477 --> 00:58:50,217
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't get me.

707
00:58:50,297 --> 00:58:50,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you do it.

708
00:58:51,887 --> 00:58:56,610
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the ultimate non-restraint, and the ultimate contradiction.

709
00:58:57,671 --> 00:59:09,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't really... you can only cover up responsibility, which means through every single act of covering up your personal responsibility, you reaffirm that personal responsibility, because that's the very reason of your actions of covering it up.

710
00:59:10,821 --> 00:59:15,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, a sātra would define it as bad faith, or inauthenticity.

711
00:59:18,106 --> 00:59:20,488
[SPEAKER_01]: But you carry it, you carry it in the heart of everything you do.

712
00:59:23,497 --> 00:59:29,081
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost like, if you want to make your society strong, you have to do your work.

713
00:59:29,682 --> 00:59:30,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

714
00:59:30,462 --> 00:59:32,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, society will be...

715
00:59:32,203 --> 00:59:37,847
[SPEAKER_01]: The strong society would be a result of personally developed individuals.

716
00:59:38,728 --> 00:59:44,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Developed in a sense of accepting responsibility and gaining perspective on account of it, as in developing wisdom.

717
00:59:45,453 --> 00:59:50,897
[SPEAKER_01]: The result would be a strong society, a resilient society, a just society.

718
00:59:52,269 --> 00:59:54,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Society that has a right perspective on things.

719
00:59:54,930 --> 00:59:57,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Society that's quite aligned with good is good and bad is bad.

720
00:59:58,490 --> 00:59:59,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what I mean.

721
00:59:59,991 --> 01:00:03,631
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that contradiction on the existential level that's bad.

722
01:00:03,691 --> 01:00:04,992
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why bad things are bad.

723
01:00:07,372 --> 01:00:20,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if now, through your own self, not through adopting like dhamma or Christianity or anything, just through that self-reflection of not tolerating existential contradiction, not covering up your responsibility for your own choices,

724
01:00:21,311 --> 01:00:29,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining perspective through constant self-questioning, basically, that will already result in you aligning yourself with good action.

725
01:00:33,215 --> 01:00:38,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily know it or have have not accepted it as some sort of external set of values.

726
01:00:42,998 --> 01:00:50,780
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if everybody were to start doing that to respective degree, as much as they're capable, it will reflect on the society as a whole.

727
01:00:52,307 --> 01:00:55,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's no society without individuals.

728
01:00:55,150 --> 01:00:56,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Individuals precede society.

729
01:00:57,592 --> 01:01:09,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, it's much more inspiring, so to speak, and it certainly requires less work to just identify with the impersonal notion of society.

730
01:01:10,387 --> 01:01:16,312
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so, enable yourself even further to cover up your own responsibility that you have been avoiding.

731
01:01:16,812 --> 01:01:21,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were transparent to begin with, you wouldn't have identified yourself, because you realize what a contradiction that is.

732
01:01:22,436 --> 01:01:23,457
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be they.

733
01:01:24,818 --> 01:01:32,143
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how much you're absorbed in a in a greater cause and whatnot, you cannot step outside of your point of view.

734
01:01:32,804 --> 01:01:33,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Ever.

735
01:01:34,485 --> 01:01:36,647
[SPEAKER_01]: You're completely confined to that perspective.

736
01:01:38,129 --> 01:01:46,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you say, no, this is not me, this is a greater good, this is a greater service, this is a greater group, basically.

737
01:01:48,475 --> 01:01:53,358
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you really want to contribute to a group, you do so from the inside, as in every single individual.

738
01:01:55,539 --> 01:02:00,262
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't abstract the group as if it's independent of individuals.

739
01:02:03,453 --> 01:02:06,096
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically an attempt at a shortcut.

740
01:02:06,176 --> 01:02:15,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, let's create a philosophy or a policy or a political system that would be dealing with group as such, putting individuals second.

741
01:02:16,907 --> 01:02:21,752
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was the definition of communism, or certainly one of the principles, maybe not the definition.

742
01:02:22,753 --> 01:02:26,517
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like, okay, well, you theorized the perfection of a group,

743
01:02:27,944 --> 01:02:28,544
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you try.

744
01:02:28,604 --> 01:02:28,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

745
01:02:28,924 --> 01:02:40,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order to maintain this perfect image of a group, we'll be sort of attacking individuals and dealing with them, because we want to... And all you're doing is maintaining your own notion, your own perspective, what you think group is.

746
01:02:41,566 --> 01:02:41,987
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

747
01:02:42,007 --> 01:02:44,867
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of your personal point of view, ever.

748
01:02:48,368 --> 01:02:50,248
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do, it's in order to cover it up.

749
01:02:51,208 --> 01:02:55,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence the group identity, group politics, and mob mentality.

750
01:02:56,989 --> 01:03:02,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Nationalism, anything, religion, if it's done for those reasons, and it is, usually.

751
01:03:04,031 --> 01:03:09,032
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be stepping outside of your group if you're not running away from your own self, self-responsibility.

752
01:03:10,272 --> 01:03:11,073
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't need a group.

753
01:03:12,693 --> 01:03:16,334
[SPEAKER_00]: I wonder how society would look if everyone took personal responsibility.

754
01:03:17,194 --> 01:03:23,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as I said, it would be, it would probably be much, much more aligned with principles of good and principles of bad.

755
01:03:24,790 --> 01:03:33,517
[SPEAKER_01]: People would have that on... on an average, sort of... an average person would have a good... would have a good sort of judgment of what's good, what's bad.

756
01:03:34,197 --> 01:03:37,480
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, like, he would have a notion of it, why good is good, bad is bad.

757
01:03:38,721 --> 01:03:41,183
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see how modern society, how far it's removed from that.

758
01:03:42,424 --> 01:03:49,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people would know and value certain things, but that's far from having... having... that's far from saying that they understood those things.

759
01:03:54,287 --> 01:03:58,390
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha was talking about the just leader back in the day, the righteous king.

760
01:03:59,710 --> 01:04:06,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Clearly, he's developed knowledge of good is good, bad is bad, and then he wouldn't be just taking and conquering, be giving back.

761
01:04:06,575 --> 01:04:16,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, people can have a certain certain quality to their life, not just exploitation for the sake of personal gain.

762
01:04:17,222 --> 01:04:22,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, then people start valuing that, and then they start doing the same, because the king is just, they are the just ones.

763
01:04:23,282 --> 01:04:26,724
[SPEAKER_01]: And they don't blame you gratuitously or try to avoid their own responsibility.

764
01:04:26,764 --> 01:04:26,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

765
01:04:27,405 --> 01:04:30,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, that's how people already are acting, responsibly and so on.

766
01:04:31,007 --> 01:04:32,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you would really stand out.

767
01:04:35,170 --> 01:04:40,653
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a decline of society, basically, when sensuality and avoiding of responsibility become a norm.

768
01:04:42,375 --> 01:04:49,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize that, like in schools, for example, when you were growing up, when I was growing up, I mean, we were taught everything and nothing.

769
01:04:50,380 --> 01:04:50,540
[SPEAKER_01]: But,

770
01:04:52,087 --> 01:05:04,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Has any of us ever been taught in our schools, elementary or higher, like, you know, the value of patience, the value of sense restraint, the value of being able to say no to yourself, to be... No.

771
01:05:05,344 --> 01:05:06,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything...

772
01:05:06,425 --> 01:05:08,748
[SPEAKER_01]: If you got any of that, it was circumstantial.

773
01:05:11,831 --> 01:05:13,232
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think it's quite universal.

774
01:05:13,372 --> 01:05:31,041
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think... like the whole notion of school, back in, you know, the days of the ancient Greece, was to part... sort of to enable people to go through their life more wisely, to be able to deal, to basically to create a perspective for them, they will then create in a better life for them.

775
01:05:31,081 --> 01:05:33,122
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize today, there's none of that.

776
01:05:33,522 --> 01:05:34,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Perspective?

777
01:05:34,303 --> 01:05:34,443
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

778
01:05:35,143 --> 01:05:38,405
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just being thrown information by the teacher,

779
01:05:39,833 --> 01:05:41,994
[SPEAKER_01]: If you peddle the information back, you're passed.

780
01:05:42,074 --> 01:05:45,515
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't, very little understanding of it is actually encouraged.

781
01:05:45,555 --> 01:05:45,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

782
01:05:45,775 --> 01:05:47,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people who are teaching haven't understood it themselves.

783
01:05:48,515 --> 01:05:50,336
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just going through a curriculum.

784
01:05:50,356 --> 01:06:06,521
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you can't be surprised that then the value on the level of society is just a mirroring personal values, which is just following your desires, getting what you want whenever you want it, however you want it, not being responsible, blaming others.

785
01:06:06,541 --> 01:06:07,921
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of a norm nowadays.

786
01:06:16,122 --> 01:06:18,183
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's impossible, the perfect society.

787
01:06:18,583 --> 01:06:19,464
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible, yeah.

788
01:06:19,884 --> 01:06:24,167
[SPEAKER_01]: The perfect society would be basically a saṅgha of arahants, but there wouldn't be a society anymore.

789
01:06:28,049 --> 01:06:38,175
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean, of course, that a person should not be aiming at perfection internally, personally.

790
01:06:38,195 --> 01:06:44,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Because aiming at 100% will at least mirror 20% into society of good, that then other people can learn from.

791
01:06:45,923 --> 01:06:48,064
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the goal, it's not to perfect society.

792
01:06:48,104 --> 01:06:56,347
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, that would be... Basically, like, think of society as a... as like a generated image on account of operating individuals.

793
01:06:57,367 --> 01:07:00,588
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's that operation of those individuals, their actions, that's the problem.

794
01:07:01,449 --> 01:07:04,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Generating image of it is just a symptom of a problem.

795
01:07:04,630 --> 01:07:10,632
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see the... you could basically see the problems of individuals by looking at the problems of society.

796
01:07:10,672 --> 01:07:12,813
[SPEAKER_01]: It would just be a more general projection of it.

797
01:07:13,767 --> 01:07:20,450
[SPEAKER_01]: By you trying to deal with the problems of society and the level of society, it's like trying to deal with the projection of a problem, not the actual problem.

798
01:07:21,870 --> 01:07:34,375
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's... you can use it to learn from it, to maintain perspective, but the problem is undone on the personal level, through that responsibility and self-reflection, and questioning, and natural virtue that will come out of it.

799
01:07:35,196 --> 01:07:36,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the projection will change.

800
01:07:36,976 --> 01:07:40,398
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be projecting those things, not the lack of those things.

801
01:07:41,995 --> 01:07:48,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

802
01:07:49,020 --> 01:07:55,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's replace this kind of arrangement of society with another arrangement, with a different system.

803
01:07:56,747 --> 01:07:57,868
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's missing the point.

804
01:07:57,908 --> 01:08:00,170
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just basically fighting over types of projection.

805
01:08:01,210 --> 01:08:05,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Or trying... fighting over ways you should be dealing with projections.

806
01:08:06,835 --> 01:08:10,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not uprooting the actual problem that is being projected.

807
01:08:13,237 --> 01:08:18,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, that attitude itself is a projection of the individual attitude towards your own problems.

808
01:08:19,818 --> 01:08:31,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when people suffer, when people experience difficulties, they don't necessarily look into themselves as in, is this a projection of my attitude or my actions?

809
01:08:31,541 --> 01:08:31,681
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

810
01:08:32,061 --> 01:08:32,921
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to manage it.

811
01:08:33,201 --> 01:08:34,222
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to manage the problem.

812
01:08:34,242 --> 01:08:35,182
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to remove the problem.

813
01:08:35,202 --> 01:08:36,362
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to get rid of the problem.

814
01:08:38,277 --> 01:08:38,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

815
01:08:39,157 --> 01:08:46,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything on the level of society is a projection of the... from the level of individuals, whichever prevails.

816
01:08:49,219 --> 01:08:57,361
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in itself is already a projection of the individual attitude that they have... that people have in regard to their own problems and suffering.

817
01:09:00,182 --> 01:09:00,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I have a problem.

818
01:09:01,482 --> 01:09:02,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, who caused it?

819
01:09:03,255 --> 01:09:07,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if it's me, okay, well, then I'll either apologize or let's pretend it never happened.

820
01:09:07,876 --> 01:09:14,218
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things are management of the problem that has already arisen.

821
01:09:14,238 --> 01:09:17,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can do that, but that's not uprooting the problem.

822
01:09:17,239 --> 01:09:18,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't confuse the two.

823
01:09:18,799 --> 01:09:27,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Uprooting of the problem is then reflecting further and saying, okay, but will I in the future then cause the same issue for myself through my careless actions, for example?

824
01:09:28,782 --> 01:09:30,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Or will I be able to prevent it?

825
01:09:31,583 --> 01:09:32,623
[SPEAKER_01]: How can I prevent it?

826
01:09:34,555 --> 01:09:53,330
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a true definition of learning from your own mistakes, like trial and error, where you want to look beyond, not just to deal with this particular issue that has arisen, but to basically extrapolate a principle that you can discern, that can then help you not make the same mistake in the future.

827
01:09:53,750 --> 01:10:03,198
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's your attitude towards your own personal existence and actions and responsibility and choices and so on, and that's the attitude of other people as well, that will be reflected in society then.

828
01:10:08,635 --> 01:10:12,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Or at least not the largest society, but your family.

829
01:10:12,257 --> 01:10:14,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, of course, yeah, yeah, certainly, yeah, yeah.

830
01:10:15,339 --> 01:10:18,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Individual, then the family, then the smaller group, whatever, then obviously.

831
01:10:20,042 --> 01:10:24,704
[SPEAKER_01]: But, but it all trickles down, like a, like a, like a stream on a mountaintop.

832
01:10:25,465 --> 01:10:34,510
[SPEAKER_01]: It's filling down lower streams, that are filling down lower rivers, that are filling down lower lakes, and other rivers, and then eventually filling down the seas, and so on.

833
01:10:36,273 --> 01:10:42,875
[SPEAKER_01]: If you dry it up at the top, or if you remove the pollution from it at the top, eventually, even the sea will cease to be polluted.

834
01:10:44,475 --> 01:10:54,638
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you're just trying to deal with the level of... on the level of the sea, without addressing the source of the pollution, well, you're fighting a losing battle.

